3.2.1 withdraw can fail unexpectedly

Submitted by Amaechi Okolobi, also found by zigtur and Koolex
Severity: Medium Risk
Context: lib.rs#L400
Description: In Lockbox::withdraw the call can revert unexpectedly due to a missing call to
whirlpool::collect_rewards. The function ﬁrst calls whirlpool::cpi::update_fees_and_rewards(cpi_-
ctx_update_fees)?;
This calls the following code in whirlpool:
let (position_update, reward_infos) = calculate_fee_and_reward_growths(
  whirlpool,
  position,
  &ctx.accounts.tick_array_lower,
  &ctx.accounts.tick_array_upper,
  timestamp,
)?;

whirlpool.update_rewards(reward_infos, timestamp);
position.update(&position_update);

This will apply rewards in certain cases. The issue is that the withdraw function doesn't then call
whirlpool::collect_rewards, meaning the position may still have rewards.
If this is the case then the whirlpool::cpi::close_position(cpi_ctx_close_position)?;‘ will fail due to this check:
if !Position::is_position_empty(&ctx.accounts.position) {
  return Err(ErrorCode::ClosePositionNotEmpty.into());
}

Which executes the following check here:
pub fn is_position_empty<'info>(position: &Position) -> bool {
  let fees_not_owed = position.fee_owed_a == 0 && position.fee_owed_b == 0;
  let mut rewards_not_owed = true;
  for i in 0..NUM_REWARDS {
    rewards_not_owed = rewards_not_owed && position.reward_infos[i].amount_owed == 0
  }
  position.liquidity == 0 && fees_not_owed && rewards_not_owed
}

So, if there are rewards owed then the withdraw call will fail, however due to the absent
whirlpool::collect_rewards call then this will DOS a user.
Recommendation: Call whirlpool::collect_rewards before closing the position.
Olas: The audited OLAS-WSOL whirlpool is originally setup without any reward incentives. The only way
these incentives might be triggered is if they are enabled by the superowner role of Orca protocol itself,
in a very rare event in order to stimulate the migration of the pool. However, that would unlikely to be
performed without communication with the OLAS protocol. Thus, the possibility of such an event is close
to zero.

